          {
            "id": 507,
            "sort": 1,
            "name": "Introduction",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"YP3XdinBZozo9Qx3V4elcqv1gBd\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-OVWJdCHrroE4zAxCBvdlqHhngac\">\n<div data-page-id=\"YP3XdinBZozo9Qx3V4elcqv1gBd\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-Z9T0dxzXIotXQDxBTTcl4DlwgEg\">The line graph illustrates the nitrogen oxide emissions, measured in grams per kilometer, produced by four different types of vehicles at various speeds ranging from 10 to 130 kilometers per hour.</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>",
            "question_id": 13814,
            "is_toggle_on": true
          },
          {
            "id": 508,
            "sort": 2,
            "name": "Overview",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"YP3XdinBZozo9Qx3V4elcqv1gBd\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-TzDtdAZfhodH2PxsXGalt84Ugke\">Overall, while diesel and petrol cars showed a U-shaped pattern in their emission levels, with initial decreases followed by increases at higher speeds, lorries and buses maintained relatively stable emissions throughout the speed range. Additionally, diesel cars consistently produced the highest levels of nitrogen oxide, while buses generated the lowest amounts.</div>\n</div>",
            "question_id": 13814,
            "is_toggle_on": true
          },
          {
            "id": 509,
            "sort": 3,
            "name": "Body 1",
            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"YP3XdinBZozo9Qx3V4elcqv1gBd\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-EG74d51RXodgDcxK17kldEBkgEc\">Looking at the cars with a U-shaped pattern, diesel vehicles started at approximately 40 g/km at 10 kph, notably higher than petrol cars which began at around 32 g/km. The release from diesel cars then declined to their lowest point of about 25 g/km at 60 kph, before rising steadily to reach roughly 42 g/km at maximum speed. Similarly, petrol cars followed this pattern but at lower levels, dropping to approximately 15 g/km at 90 kph before climbing to about 22 g/km at 130 kph.</div>\n</div>",
